4,4,8,8,8,3,3,3,3,3,3],[3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,4,4,4,8,8,8,3,3,3,3,3,3],[3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,4,4,4,8,8,8,3,3,3,3,3,3],[3,3,3,3,3,3,8,4,3,3,8,8,8,3,3,3,8,8,8,3,3,3],[3,3,3,3,3,8,3,8,3,3,8,8,8,3,3,3,8,8,8,3,3,3],[3,3,3,3,3,1,8,3,3,3,8,8,8,3,3,3,8,8,8,3,3,3],[3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,8,8,8,1,1,1,3,3,3],[3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,8,8,8,1,1,1,3,3,3],[3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,8,8,8,1,1,1,3,3,3],[3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3],[3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3],[3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3],[3,1,8,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,8,8,1,1,3,3,3,3,3],[3,8,3,8,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,8,8,1,1,3,3,3,3,3],[3,3,8,4,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,8,8,3,3,8,8,3,3,3,3,3],[3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,8,8,3,3,8,8,3,3,3,3,3],[3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,4,4,8,8,3,3,3,3,3,3,3],[3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,4,4,8,8,3,3,3,3,3,3,3],[3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3],[3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3],[3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3]]}]},"teaching_variant":"canonical","teacher_models":["claude-opus-5-5"],"explanation":"The grid is made of one small multicolour **template** plus several **marker blocks**.\n\n**What the template is.** Each input has one 3×3 cluster of mixed colours:\n- Pair 1: `1 1 6 / 1 4 1 / 2 1 1`\n- Pair 2: `2 8 8 / 8 8 1 / 8 1 3`\n- Pair 3: `2 3 8 / 3 3 3 / 8 3 4`\n\nTwo of its colours sit at opposite corners and also appear elsewhere in the grid as solid squares. These are the **markers**: 6 and 2 in pair 1, 2 and 3 in pair 2, 2 and 4 in pair 3. The remaining colour is the **body**: 1, 8 and 3 respectively. Background-coloured cells inside the template, like the centre 4 in pair 1, are holes and are never painted.\n\n**What the output adds.** Wherever two marker squares of the same size s sit where the template's two corners would land, the output paints the body around them. The template is scaled by s and reoriented by one of the 8 rotations or flips. I checked this on each pair:\n\n- **Pair 1.** The 2 square is at rows 7–9, columns 11–13 and the 6 square at rows 13–15, columns 5–7, so s = 3. The painted 9×9 area reads `1 1 2 / 1 4 1 / 6 1 1` at block level. That is the template rotated 180°, with the 4 hole left empty.\n- **Pair 2.** The single cells 2 at (1,13) and 3 at (3,15) get an unscaled copy in the original orientation. The 2×2 squares (2 at the top-right, 3 at the bottom-left) get a ×2 copy reading `8 8 2 / 1 8 8 / 3 1 8`. That is the template flipped horizontally.\n- **Pair 3.** Both pairs of single cells (4 with 2) get unscaled, reoriented copies.\n\n**Ambiguity.** Sometimes two orientations fit the same marker pair. In every case here the template is symmetric about the relevant diagonal, so both choices paint the same cells.\n\n**Algorithm.**\n1. Take the largest multicolour object (diagonal connectivity) as the template.\n2. Marker colours are template colours that also occur outside it; the rest is the body.\n3. For each of the 8 orientations, each marker square of colour c with side s, and each template cell of colour c, compute the implied scaled placement.\n4. Accept the placement only if every marker cell of the oriented template lands exactly on an existing solid square object of the right colour and size.\n5. Paint the body blocks onto background cells.\n\n**Test input.** The template is `3 8 4 / 8 3 8 / 1 8 3`. Its markers are 4 and 1 and its body is 8. Three placements are found:\n- the 3×3 squares (4 at the top-left, 1 at the bottom-right), scale 3;\n- the 2×2 squares (1 at the top-right, 4 at the bottom-left), scale 2;\n- the single cells 1 at (13,1) and 4 at (15,3), scale 1.\n\nEach placement is filled with 8, and the template's 3-valued cells stay as background.","code":"def transform(grid):\n    g = np.array(grid)\n    bg = background(g)\n    out = g.copy()\n    multi = objects(g, bg=bg, diagonal=True, multicolour=True)\n    tobj = max(multi, key=lambda o: (len(set(g[y, x] for y, x in o[\"cells\"])), o[\"size\"]))\n    ty0, tx0, ty1, tx1 = tobj[\"box\"]\n    tmpl = g[ty0:ty1+1, tx0:tx1+1].copy()\n    tcells = set(tobj[\"cells\"])\n    tcols = set(int(v) for v in tmpl.flatten()) - {bg}\n    outside = set()\n    for y in range(g.shape[0]):\n        for x in range(g.shape[1]):\n            if (y, x) not in tcells and g[y, x] != bg:\n                outside.add(int(g[y, x]))\n    markers = tcols & outside\n    singles = [o for o in objects(g, bg=bg) if set(o[\"cells\"]).isdisjoint(tcells)]\n    blocks = {}\n    for o in singles:\n        y0, x0, y1, x1 = o[\"box\"]\n        if y1 - y0 == x1 - x0 and o[\"size\"] == (y1-y0+1)**2:\n            blocks[(y0, x0, y1-y0+1)] = o[\"colour\"]\n    syms = []\n    t = tmpl\n    for k in range(4):\n        r = np.rot90(t, k)\n        syms.append(r); syms.append(np.fliplr(r))\n    H, W = g.shape\n    done = set()\n    for T in syms:\n        mpos = [(r, c, int(T[r, c])) for r in range(T.shape[0]) for c in range(T.shape[1]) if int(T[r, c]) in markers]\n        if not mpos:\n            continue\n        for (by, bx, s), col in blocks.items():\n            for (r, c, mc) in mpos:\n                if mc != col:\n                    continue\n                oy, ox = by - r*s, bx - c*s\n                ok = all(blocks.get((oy+rr*s, ox+cc*s, s)) == m2 for rr, cc, m2 in mpos)\n                if not ok:\n                    continue\n                if oy < 0 or ox < 0 or oy + T.shape[0]*s > H or ox + T.shape[1]*s > W:\n                    continue\n                key = (oy, ox, s, T.tobytes())\n                if key in done:\n                    continue\n                done.add(key)\n                changed = False\n                for rr in range(T.shape[0]):\n                    for cc in range(T.shape[1]):\n                        v = int(T[rr, cc])\n                        if v == bg or v in markers:\n                            continue\n                        for dy in range(s):\n                            for dx in range(s):\n                                yy, xx = oy+rr*s+dy, ox+cc*s+dx\n                                if out[yy, xx] == bg:\n                                    out[yy, xx] = v\n                                    changed = True\n                if changed:\n                    emit(out, f\"Stamp template (scale {s}) between markers at origin ({oy},{ox}), filling body colour.\")\n    emit(out, \"Final: every marker pair completed with the scaled, reoriented template.\")\n    return out\n","candidate_tactics":{"objects":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"Take the largest multicolour object (diagonal connectivity) as the template."}],"selection":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"**What the output adds.** Wherever two marker squares of the same size s sit where the template's two corners would land, the output paints the body around them."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"Take the largest multicolour object (diagonal connectivity) as the template."}],"rigid":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"The template is scaled by s and reoriented by one of the 8 rotations or flips."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"That is the template rotated 180°, with the 4 hole left empty."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"In every case here the template is symmetric about the relevant diagonal, so both choices paint the same cells."}],"template":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"The grid is made of one small multicolour **template** plus several **marker blocks**."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"**What the template is.** Each input has one 3×3 cluster of mixed colours:"},{"source":"final explanation","text":"Background-coloured cells inside the template, like the centre 4 in pair 1, are holes and are never painted."}],"topology":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"Background-coloured cells inside the template, like the centre 4 in pair 1, are holes and are never painted."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"That is the template rotated 180°, with the 4 hole left empty."}],"scale":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"The template is scaled by s and reoriented by one of the 8 rotations or flips."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"each marker square of colour c with side s, and each template cell of colour c, compute the implied scaled placement."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"- the 3×3 squares (4 at the top-left, 1 at the bottom-right), scale 3;"}]},"provenance":{"reference_assisted":false,"query_labels_used_for_rule_diagnosis":false},"artifact_sha256":"d9891586f7bc910ab89aa53bc3619bff470cc10e37fba2c2b160633727d1f947","task_sha256":"cfafc23aac272cb4f8eaf2c248f95e42cdc15a3868894814e312bf4a053ae9b4","validation":"Frozen corpus recorded successful replay on teaching fixtures; synthetic generalization not established."}
